  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday through Tuesday...\n\nAn upper trough and surface low will impact the region today,\nbringing precipitation and strong winds across Southern Montana \nand Northern Wyoming. As the trough axis pivots through Central \nMontana, precipitation will spread across the forecast area. Rain \nand snow are forecast, with much of the lower elevations \nexperiencing rainfall. Meanwhile, snowfall is expected for all \nmountains and light accumulations in the foothills. The \nBeartooth/Red Lodge Foothills have the best chance to see a tenth\nof an inch or two of snowfall into this afternoon. Locally higher\nsnowfall amounts are forecast along the Bozeman Pass and higher \nlocations within the Paradise Valley. More on this to come.\n\nAs the surface low progresses through the southeast today, winds\nwill increase across the area with gusts in the 30s and 40s mph.\nHigher gusts in the 40s to 50s mph are expected along US-191 from\nBig Timber through Judith Gap as well as far Eastern Montana \nalong the Dakotas Border. There is a period this afternoon where \n850-700mb winds increase towards 50 knots across far Eastern \nMontana. Precipitation is likely to be occurring when these \nstrongest low-mid level winds arrive. Typically this would hinder \nthe threat for high winds, though enough mixing may bring these \nwinds to the surface with gusts approaching 60 mph. As a result, a\nHigh Wind Warning is in effect until 9 PM MST this evening for \nFallon and Carter Counties.\n\nCircling back on the wintry precipitation now, the latest forecast\nhas snowfall amounts of 3 to 6 inches for west aspects of the \nAbsaroka/Beartooth and Crazy Mountains through this afternoon.\nLocally greater amounts up to 8 inches are possible for the higher\nelevations of the Absaroka/Beartooths. For the Paradise Valley, \nLivingston Area, and Northern Park County up to an inch of snow \nis forecast. Additionally, locally higher amounts up to 6 inches \nare possible atop the Bozeman Pass. For the Judith Gap area, a \nbrief period of northerly winds will accompany the initial push of\nprecipitation. The combination of falling wet snow and gusty \nwinds may result in hazardous travel along US-191 through Judith \nGap this morning. Because of this, a Winter Weather Advisory \nremains in effect for the Judith Gap area until 11 AM MST.\n\nTotal precipitation amounts forecast for the area are in the range\nof a few hundredths to nearly two tenths of an inch. The greatest\nprecipitation totals amongst the lower elevations exist over\nsouth-central Montana. The Pryor and Bighorn Mountains will also \npick up a few inches of snow, with the highest elevations of the \nBighorns seeing up to 8 inches. The latest model trends indicate \nprecipitation could be over as soon as the early afternoon as the \ntrough quickly ejects into the Dakotas. Expect winds to diminish \nfrom west to east late this afternoon and through the evening.\n\nShortwave ridging arrives for Thursday, temperatures will not\nrespond to this however, with highs in the 30s and low 40sF in the\nwake of today's system. Friday onward, uncertainty remains with\nthe evolution of a system along the West Coast. The general\nconsensus from the latest ensembles is that a trough will dig \ninto the Four Corners as another potential system begins to drop \nthrough Alberta. What this means for the Northern Rockies is\nunclear, as the further south the parent trough digs the lesser \nany impacts would be for our area. Confidence is greatest that \naround to slightly below normal temperatures will remain in the \nforecast through the weekend, with highs in the 30s and 40sF.\n\nMatos",
